WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.590
On 22 October 1956, students at a Budapest university drew up sixteen demands.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:16.280
They wanted Soviet troops out, the work norms revised, the farm deliveries changed and the old coat of arms back.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:22.100
Behind every point lay something that millions of Hungarians knew from their own lives.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:29.320
The next day, their march became a revolution. To see why, we go back seven years.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:44.190
By 1949, Hungary was a one-party state, run by Mátyás Rákosi and his Hungarian Workers' Party.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.930
Its fist was the State Protection Authority, the ÁVH, the political police.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:57.600
It worked from 60 Andrássy út in Budapest, a building the fascist Arrow Cross party had used before it.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:06.640
From 1950 it answered to the government on paper, but in practice to Rákosi alone, with Soviet advisers at its side.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:17.960
Between 1950 and 1953, by the 1956 Institute's count, it took proceedings against some 650,000 people.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:26.480
That was in a country of about 9.2 million. Its informers sat in offices, factories, schools and blocks of flats.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:34.680
No one knew, witnesses later told the UN, where their words would be repeated half an hour later.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:42.100
A law of 1952 even punished anyone who shunned an informer, with up to six months in prison.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.970
In its cells, prisoners were beaten, starved and threatened until they signed confessions.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:59.020
Not even Communists were safe. In 1949 László Rajk, a former interior minister, was executed on invented charges.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:10.610
The ÁVH did not even need a court. It could simply intern people, holding them without any trial.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:17.780
Peasants branded kulaks, Social Democrats, churchmen and so-called class aliens were held in camps.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:25.500
There were camps at Kistarcsa, Tiszalök and Kazincbarcika, and the most feared of all, Recsk, in the Mátra hills.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:36.790
At this secret camp, opened in 1950, some 1,200 to 1,300 men at a time broke stone in a quarry, by former prisoners' counts.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:47.640
Whole families were taken too. Before dawn on 23 June 1950, ÁVH men and police woke families near the southern and western borders.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:58.210
That night 2,446 people were taken to closed labour camps on the Hortobágy plain. By 1953 there were twelve.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:07.780
When the camps closed in 1953, official records counted 7,281 people released, and more had been taken.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:19.780
In 1951 the regime turned on Budapest itself, expelling 12,704 people, former nobles, officers and officials with their families.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:28.280
They were sent to 138 villages in the east and lodged in strangers' houses, often a kulák family's.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.360
And children labelled class aliens could be barred from further study.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:45.180
Meanwhile the regime set out to turn a farming country into a land of iron and steel.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:54.320
The first five-year plan began in 1950. After war broke out in Korea, Stalin demanded more arms, and the targets soared.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:05.110
Planned investment rose from 27.6 to 85 billion forints, and about nine-tenths of industry's share went to heavy industry.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:10.960
In the factories, pay depended on the norm, the output that counted as a day's work.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:20.430
The norms kept rising. At the Ikarus bus works, the new norms of 1950 cut average pay by 35.5 percent.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:26.920
Workers were also pressed to lend the state at least a month's pay every year, in so-called Peace Loans.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.940
From January 1951, six years after the war, food was rationed again.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:42.080
By 1952, real wages had fallen to about two-thirds of their pre-war level, by the historian Ignác Romsics's figures.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:51.130
In 1954 the trade unions' own survey found a third of wage earners below the subsistence minimum.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:04.000
In the villages, peasants owed the state compulsory deliveries of grain, meat and other produce, at low fixed prices.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.820
The quota was set by the land, not by the harvest, so a bad year did not lower it.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:22.240
Anyone farming more than 25 holds, about 14 hectares, was branded a kulák, a rich peasant. In 1949 there were 61,922.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:32.840
Kulák families got the heaviest quotas. In 1951 they had to deliver goods worth 700 to 1,100 kilograms of wheat a hold.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:39.630
That year, the best of the period, the average wheat harvest was 970 kilograms a hold.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:45.500
Brigades searched cellars and lofts for hidden grain. People called it attic sweeping.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:56.050
From 1948 to 1955, almost 200,000 people were convicted of "crimes against public supply", by official statistics.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:06.170
Even István Dobi, the regime's own head of state, later said the villages had had many times more reason to complain than the towns.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:21.010
Behind the regime stood the Soviet Union. Its army had entered Hungary fighting Germany in 1944 and 1945, and stayed.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:29.050
The 1947 peace treaty let Soviet troops remain, to guard their army's lines to its zone in Austria.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:39.170
In May 1955 a treaty ended Austria's occupation. The day before, the new Warsaw Pact had given the troops a fresh reason to stay.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:50.090
Before October 1956, the UN committee was told, about 20,000 Soviet soldiers and 600 tanks were in Hungary.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:59.040
Hungary paid, too. The 1945 armistice made it deliver goods worth 200 million dollars to the Soviet Union.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:08.610
Trade deals with Moscow were kept secret. Uranium was mined near Pécs, and people believed it went to the Soviet Union.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:18.860
Soviet power showed in everyday symbols. In 1949 a Soviet-style emblem with a red star replaced the Kossuth coat of arms.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:25.730
Soldiers wore uniforms modelled on the Soviet ones, and Russian was a compulsory subject at school.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:35.100
15 March, the anniversary of the revolution of 1848, was no longer a holiday, just an ordinary working day.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:45.780
The UN committee found the Soviet soldiers themselves not personally unpopular. People resented the power that kept the regime in place.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:08:00.450
Stalin died in March 1953. Under a new prime minister, Imre Nagy, the internment camps were closed and many prisoners came home.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:05.770
But the ÁVH remained, Rákosi still led the party, and the fear stayed.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:16.890
By 1956, workers, peasants, students and writers all had grievances, and the students' sixteen points took them up almost one by one.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:24.260
Point 1 asked for all Soviet troops to leave, and point 8 for the truth about reparations and uranium.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:32.030
Points 9 and 10 asked for the norms to be revised, a minimum wage, and the deliveries put on a new basis.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:41.350
Points 11 and 12 asked for the political trials to be re-examined, for free speech, and for the personnel files to be destroyed.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:50.720
Points 13 and 14 asked for Stalin's statue to go, the Kossuth arms to return, and 15 March to be a holiday again.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.090
And everywhere, people demanded one thing more, that the ÁVH be disbanded.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:06.060
An eighteen-year-old student later put it simply to the UN: "We wanted freedom and not a good comfortable life."

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:17.210
Between Stalin's death and October 1956 lay three years of hope and disappointment. That is the road to 23 October.
